00:00:00.418 --> 00:00:03.898
Hello everyone. Welcome to the LangFocus channel 
and my name is Paul.

00:00:03.998 --> 00:00:06.318
Today's topic is the Dutch language.

00:00:06.418 --> 00:00:10.898
Dutch is a language that's spoken mainly in Europe
but also in a few other places around the world.

00:00:11.078 --> 00:00:17.678
It has 23 million native speakers  and around 28 million 
speakers in total, including second language speakers.

00:00:17.738 --> 00:00:22.178
It is most widely spoken in the Netherlands 
where it has around 16 million native speakers.

00:00:22.438 --> 00:00:27.878
It is also spoken by around 60% of the population 
of Belgium, mostly in the northern region of Flanders.

00:00:28.018 --> 00:00:30.058
That's around 6.5 million people

00:00:30.478 --> 00:00:35.598
It's also spoken by a small number of people across 
the border in French Flanders, which lies adjacent 
to Belgium.

00:00:35.738 --> 00:00:39.638
But only around 20,000 people there
still speak it on a regular basis.

00:00:39.878 --> 00:00:49.217
It is also spoken in the South American nation 
of Surinam where it has around 350,000 native speakers
as well as around 250,000 second language speakers

00:00:49.338 --> 00:00:53.818
it is also an official language on the island of Aruba, along with the language papiamento.

00:00:53.858 --> 00:00:58.358
But few of the 100,000 people there actually use Dutch, 
even though they all learn it in school.

00:00:58.537 --> 00:01:03.657
It is also spoken in Curacao where it is spoken 
by around 15,000 people as a native language

00:01:03.738 --> 00:01:07.478
and by many of the other 140,000 people 
as a second language.

00:01:07.738 --> 00:01:10.898
It is also an official language 
in Sint Maarten along with English.

00:01:10.978 --> 00:01:14.558
A couple of thousand people there speak Dutch 
but English is actually much more common.

00:01:14.677 --> 00:01:18.617
There is also Afrikaans, a language spoken 
in South Africa and Namibia

00:01:18.618 --> 00:01:22.458
which is a daughter language of Dutch
and is mutually intelligible to some extent

00:01:22.478 --> 00:01:26.958
But i'll leave Afrikaans out for now and I'll speak 
about it in a different video in the future.

00:01:27.098 --> 00:01:30.718
Dutch is a member of the West Germanic branch 
of the Germanic language family,

00:01:30.798 --> 00:01:35.098
which also includes Afrikaans, Frisian, English 
and German, among others.

00:01:35.317 --> 00:01:38.457
Dutch is one of the languages 
that is most closely related to English.

00:01:38.518 --> 00:01:43.298
Actually, Frisian is more closely related to English 
than Dutch is but Dutch is a close second.

00:01:43.398 --> 00:01:46.238
And a lot of people say that Dutch is
quite similar to German too.

00:01:46.258 --> 00:01:50.238
In fact, some people say that Dutch lies 
somewhere between English and German.

00:01:50.558 --> 00:01:54.098
Now that's not a very precise statement 
but I think there is some truth to it.

00:01:54.438 --> 00:01:55.438
The history of Dutch

00:01:55.658 --> 00:02:00.518
All Germanic languages developed from Proto-Germanic 
which was spoken around 500 BCE

00:02:00.638 --> 00:02:03.458
in northern continental Europe 
and also in Scandinavia.

00:02:03.758 --> 00:02:09.458
By the 2nd century CE, it had begun diverging 
into distinct northern, western and eastern dialects.

00:02:09.618 --> 00:02:13.978
The Western dialect is the ancestor of Dutch 
and of all of the West Germanic languages.

00:02:14.278 --> 00:02:20.898
Variation developed in the Western dialect but all of its 
varieties remained intelligible until around the 8th c. CE.

00:02:21.078 --> 00:02:25.458
But, by then, a series of sound changes have begun 
to take place that made Old High German,

00:02:25.458 --> 00:02:29.518
the ancestor of modern German, much more distinct 
from the other West Germanic languages.

00:02:29.638 --> 00:02:34.178
For more information on German and its history, 
check out my video on German right here

00:02:34.418 --> 00:02:40.118
Old Dutch, also known as Old Low Franconian, remained 
unaffected by the changes that affected Old High German.

00:02:40.238 --> 00:02:43.617
Also unaffected were :
Old Saxon, Old Frisian an Old English

00:02:44.157 --> 00:02:47.077
But those languages underwent 
a different series of sound changes.

00:02:47.298 --> 00:02:50.318
But again Dutch was mostly unaffected 
by these changes too.

00:02:51.278 --> 00:02:52.058
Middle Dutch

00:02:52.158 --> 00:02:57.818
Old Dutch developed into Middle Dutch, which was
spoken and written between 1150 and 1500 CE.

00:02:57.878 --> 00:02:59.518
It was a rich literary period.

00:02:59.558 --> 00:03:05.858
And literature from this time period is often quite 
readable for speakers of Modern Dutch,
because Dutch is quite a conservative language.

00:03:06.038 --> 00:03:09.638
Middle Dutch developed into Modern Dutch 
by the middle of the 16th century.

00:03:09.818 --> 00:03:15.597
The process of the standardization of Dutch began
in the year 1477 at the end of the Middle Dutch period.

00:03:15.658 --> 00:03:19.218
The dialects of Flanders and Brabant
were most influential at that time.

00:03:19.397 --> 00:03:25.537
Then, in the 16th century, the move to standardization 
became stronger with the Antwerp dialect 
being the most influential.

00:03:25.677 --> 00:03:33.077
In the year 1637, the first major Dutch Bible translation 
known as "Statenvertaling" had been published.
("Statenvertaling" means "translation of the States")

00:03:33.278 --> 00:03:37.918
It had been translated in such a way that people 
from all over the country could read and understand it.

00:03:38.038 --> 00:03:42.438
This Bible was read by nearly everyone and it helped
greatly in the standardization of Dutch.

00:03:42.658 --> 00:03:48.378
The southern Netherlands, now Belgium and Luxembourg 
were separate and under Spanish, Austrian and then French rule.

00:03:48.677 --> 00:03:54.797
More than half of the people in Belgium spoke a dialect 
of Dutch but French was most widely used in public life 
in schools, etc...

00:03:54.997 --> 00:03:58.238
So Dutch remained unstandardized there 
until the 19th century,

00:03:58.238 --> 00:04:01.898
when the Flemish movement started standing up 
for the rights of Dutch-speakers.

00:04:02.138 --> 00:04:04.898
They adopted the same standard
 language used in the Netherlands.

00:04:05.218 --> 00:04:10.977
Nowadays in both the Netherlands and in Flanders, 
the northern Dutch-speaking region of Belgium,
the situation is similar.

00:04:11.078 --> 00:04:15.098
There are a number of spoken varieties of Dutch 
as well as a standard language.

00:04:15.298 --> 00:04:19.898
So the difference lies mainly in pronunciation 
of standard Dutch and in the local dialects used.

00:04:20.018 --> 00:04:24.777
The dialects of Flanders tend to be more conservative 
and use more older Dutch vocabulary.

00:04:24.958 --> 00:04:25.838
Regional languages.

00:04:26.098 --> 00:04:29.258
There are Dutch dialects but there are
also different regional languages,

00:04:29.337 --> 00:04:32.257
that are West Germanic languages
and closely related to Dutch,

00:04:32.378 --> 00:04:35.998
but not as closely related as the dialects 
that are considered part of Dutch.

00:04:36.217 --> 00:04:40.717
Regional languages in the Netherlands include: 
- Frisian, in the northern province of Friesland

00:04:41.018 --> 00:04:44.578
- Low Saxon in the northeast 
and there are various dialects of Low Saxon

00:04:44.938 --> 00:04:48.178
- and Limburgish or East Low Franconian in the southeast.

00:04:48.717 --> 00:04:54.157
Low Saxon spreads across the border with Germany 
and forms part of a dialect continuum  between Dutch 
and German.

00:04:54.397 --> 00:05:00.817
If you've seen my German video, you know that 
the dialects in northern Germany are referred to as 
Low German and can be considered a separate language.

00:05:00.938 --> 00:05:05.298
The Dutch Low Saxon dialects are closely related
to the Low German dialects across the border.

00:05:05.337 --> 00:05:10.397
This is also true for Limburgish which is related to Franconian dialect spoken across the border in Germany.

00:05:10.578 --> 00:05:16.438
It should be noted that Dutch dialects
and regional languages are in decline
with standard Dutch becoming more widespread.

00:05:16.658 --> 00:05:20.958
And with some people speaking a kind of combination 
of standard Dutch with some dialectal features.

00:05:21.278 --> 00:05:22.338
So what is Dutch like?

00:05:22.578 --> 00:05:27.118
Dutch is not mutually intelligible with English 
but you will often notice cognate vocabulary.

00:05:27.217 --> 00:05:29.957
and at the most basic level, 
you will notice similar grammar,

00:05:29.958 --> 00:05:33.278
if you disregard the details and just look 
at the most basic sentences.

00:05:33.518 --> 00:05:38.418
Occasionally there are sentences in Dutch that might 
be strangely familiar to English speakers. For example:

00:05:39.597 --> 00:05:40.857
This means: "What is pour name?"

00:05:42.417 --> 00:05:43.857
This means: "My name is Luke"

00:05:45.477 --> 00:05:47.257
This means: "The bear drank beer"

00:05:47.477 --> 00:05:49.477
(The guy must have gotten that one in  Duolingo)

00:05:50.837 --> 00:05:52.837
This means: "It is not far"

00:05:54.078 --> 00:05:56.078
This means: "That is good news"

00:05:56.477 --> 00:06:02.397
Now, if all Dutch sentences were so similar to English, then the two languages could probably be 
quite intelligible with each other.

00:06:02.397 --> 00:06:07.557
but most Dutch sentences are not that similar to English.
But you will see a lot of cognate vocabulary.

00:06:07.698 --> 00:06:08.277
Grammar

00:06:08.458 --> 00:06:13.638
Similar to German, Dutch is a language that places verbs 
at the end of the sentence, after the first initial verb.

00:06:13.878 --> 00:06:19.318
So its underlying structure is basically SOV,
but the first verb is in second position after the subject.

00:06:19.538 --> 00:06:22.838
So, if there's only one verb in the sentence, 
then it's basically like SVO.

00:06:23.018 --> 00:06:25.998
Here's a sentence with one verb.

00:06:25.998 --> 00:06:27.678
This means "I am writing a letter".

00:06:27.678 --> 00:06:30.658
Word by word, this is like : " I - write - a - letter "

00:06:30.837 --> 00:06:33.037
Just like English, with the verb in second position.

00:06:33.238 --> 00:06:34.638
Here's a sentence with two verbs:

00:06:37.018 --> 00:06:39.097
This means: "I want to give you some goodies".

00:06:39.097 --> 00:06:43.377
Word by word, that is 
"I - want - you (indirect object) - some - goodies - give".

00:06:43.558 --> 00:06:46.038
In this sentence, the first verb is in second position

00:06:46.258 --> 00:06:48.258
but the next verb comes at the end of the sentence.

00:06:48.618 --> 00:06:49.118
Nouns

00:06:49.417 --> 00:06:49.917
Gender

00:06:50.277 --> 00:06:55.017
When it comes to gender, Dutch traditionally has 3 genders:
masculine, feminine and neuter

00:06:55.078 --> 00:07:01.838
But, in modern spoken Dutch, the distinction between 
masculine and feminine gender has almost become 
irrelevant, because they don't look or sound different.

00:07:02.018 --> 00:07:06.958
It used to determine which pronoun would be used 
to refer to those nouns, whether it's "he" or "she",

00:07:07.098 --> 00:07:09.178
but people generally just use "he" these days.

00:07:09.378 --> 00:07:12.618
So now the two genders are basically:
common gender and neuter.

00:07:12.838 --> 00:07:16.678
The noun's gender agrees with articles as
well as the forms of adjectives

00:07:17.078 --> 00:07:17.578
PLURAL

00:07:17.917 --> 00:07:19.817
There are two types of plural endings in Dutch

00:07:20.058 --> 00:07:22.478
One is "-en" and the other is "-s"

00:07:22.678 --> 00:07:27.618
There are some - well, not rules but - patterns for when 
to use each one but there are a lot of exceptions.

00:07:27.737 --> 00:07:29.057
So you have words like :

00:07:29.058 --> 00:07:31.018
Deur - DeurEN  = "Door(s)"

00:07:31.217 --> 00:07:32.977
Boot - BOTEN  = "Boat(s)"

00:07:33.198 --> 00:07:34.337
and you have words like:

00:07:34.678 --> 00:07:36.418
Sleutel - SleutelS = "Key(s)"

00:07:36.678 --> 00:07:38.578
Lepel - LepelS = "spoon(s)"

00:07:38.878 --> 00:07:42.198
A few neuter nouns have a plural form "-eren". 
For example:

00:07:42.338 --> 00:07:44.158
Kind - KindEREN = "child - children"

00:07:44.657 --> 00:07:45.217
Cases

00:07:45.417 --> 00:07:47.937
Dutch used to have noun cases similar to German.

00:07:47.938 --> 00:07:51.378
There were four cases: 
nominative, genitive, dative and accusative.

00:07:51.538 --> 00:07:57.038
These were part of standard written Dutch until 
the 1940s but they were dropped because 
nobody use them in speech anymore.

00:08:00.958 --> 00:08:03.058
There are several different articles in Dutch.

00:08:03.058 --> 00:08:05.178
There is an indefinite article : "een"

00:08:05.337 --> 00:08:08.057
And there are definite articles for singular 
and for plural nouns.

00:08:08.318 --> 00:08:10.258
For singular masculine: "de"

00:08:10.558 --> 00:08:12.498
For singular feminine: "de"

00:08:12.777 --> 00:08:14.457
For singular neuter : "het"

00:08:14.737 --> 00:08:18.017
And the definite article for plural for all genders : "de"

00:08:18.378 --> 00:08:21.898
An interesting thing is there is a negative article: "geen"

00:08:22.058 --> 00:08:24.938
This is the same for all genders and
for both singular and plural.

00:08:25.237 --> 00:08:25.777
Verbs

00:08:26.137 --> 00:08:29.857
There are two verb tenses in Dutch :
past and present, or non past.

00:08:30.118 --> 00:08:34.518
But saying there are two tenses is kind of misleading,
because, similar to in English, the verb can be used

00:08:34.518 --> 00:08:38.258
in combination with different auxiliary verbs 
to express different meanings.

00:08:38.378 --> 00:08:42.398
And there are participial forms that can be
used to show passive or continuous actions

00:08:42.678 --> 00:08:43.418
For example:

00:08:43.698 --> 00:08:45.538
Here's a sentence using the simple past:

00:08:47.137 --> 00:08:48.797
This means "He built a house"

00:08:48.997 --> 00:08:51.277
So we start with the infinitive "bouwen"

00:08:51.398 --> 00:08:54.938
And this is the verb stem "bouw"
and this is the past tense form : "bouwde"

00:08:55.517 --> 00:08:58.717
Now here's a present perfect sentence:
"Hij heeft een huis gebouwd"

00:08:58.958 --> 00:09:00.618
This means:  "he has built a house"

00:09:00.898 --> 00:09:02.778
so we start with the infinitive "hebben"

00:09:02.958 --> 00:09:06.258
and here's the verb stem : "heb" 
and here's the past tense "heeft"

00:09:06.698 --> 00:09:08.938
and the second verb is a past participle

00:09:09.158 --> 00:09:12.358
This is formed by adding the prefix "ge-" and the suffix "-d"

00:09:12.578 --> 00:09:18.898
Notice again that the second verb is at the end of 
the sentence, even though the first verb is
in second position after the subject.

00:09:19.138 --> 00:09:21.458
There are three different ways to
express the future tense:

00:09:21.798 --> 00:09:26.658
One of them is to use this auxiliary verb "zullen" 
This means something like "shall" or "will".

00:09:28.898 --> 00:09:30.898
The sentence means "I will do it tomorrow".

00:09:30.898 --> 00:09:35.198
You can also use this verb 
meaning "to go" ("gaan") plus the infinitive

00:09:35.438 --> 00:09:37.638
This is similar to using "going to" in English.

00:09:38.017 --> 00:09:38.517
For example:

00:09:40.217 --> 00:09:41.757
This means "It's going to rain"

00:09:42.778 --> 00:09:45.038
And you can also use the present tense for the future.

00:09:47.278 --> 00:09:49.278
This means "They won't come until later"

00:09:49.278 --> 00:09:51.358
Literally "They come only later"

00:09:51.918 --> 00:09:55.378
So the verb system in Dutch is rather similar 
to the one used in English.

00:09:55.398 --> 00:09:59.418
The main difference being that any verb 
after the first one comes at the end.

00:09:59.678 --> 00:10:00.418
Pronunciation

00:10:00.658 --> 00:10:03.418
One of the hardest parts of learning
Dutch might be the pronunciation

00:10:03.617 --> 00:10:06.157
because there are some sounds 
that can be a challenge for learners.

00:10:06.357 --> 00:10:09.957
And native speakers of dutch seem quite 
conscious of the challenges we face.

00:10:10.158 --> 00:10:13.198
That was kind of an indirect way of saying:
"they think our pronunciation sucks"

00:10:13.477 --> 00:10:17.977
Some of the sounds that require special effort 
are the Dutch G sound. For example:

00:10:19.217 --> 00:10:22.037
and the sound which "CH" often represents. 
For example:

00:10:23.318 --> 00:10:26.278
Notice that this is the same sound
as the one represented by G.

00:10:27.217 --> 00:10:30.078
But in other cases, CH represents the "ch" sound

00:10:30.298 --> 00:10:33.938
and sometimes those sounds come at the beginning of 
a word which is a little bit of a challenge

00:10:34.058 --> 00:10:38.438
but sometimes, they come right after the letter S 
like in the word for "school"

00:10:38.938 --> 00:10:40.938
This sound combination is quite common in Dutch.

00:10:41.158 --> 00:10:45.298
There are a lot of long words in Dutch.
Some of them can be over 30 characters long.

00:10:45.578 --> 00:10:47.357
Here are a couple extreme examples:

00:10:50.058 --> 00:10:54.258
This one means "multiple personality disorder"
and that is 35 characters long.

00:10:57.837 --> 00:11:03.217
This one means: "preparation activities for a children's carnival procession" and this is 53 characters long.

00:11:03.758 --> 00:11:05.998
And Dutch has also has a lot of colorful idioms. 
For example:

00:11:08.398 --> 00:11:10.218
This means "make the cat wise"

00:11:10.517 --> 00:11:15.597
This means that someone is saying something so 
outlandish or unbelievable that even the cat won't believe it.

00:11:15.938 --> 00:11:16.698
Another example:

00:11:18.578 --> 00:11:20.578
This means "he has a beard and his throat".

00:11:20.877 --> 00:11:25.757
This idiom describes the situation when a boy 
reaches puberty and his voice starts to change.

00:11:26.017 --> 00:11:27.197
How hard is Dutch to learn?

00:11:27.458 --> 00:11:30.158
For English speakers, a lot of Dutch will seem strangely familiar.

00:11:30.298 --> 00:11:31.258
And of course that will help.

00:11:31.258 --> 00:11:35.558
But there are some issues in learning the pronunciation 
as well as the finer details of grammar.

00:11:35.738 --> 00:11:40.818
But maybe the hardest thing about learning Dutch is that 
Dutch speakers usually speak English very well.

00:11:40.818 --> 00:11:42.338
So it will be hard to practice.

00:11:42.438 --> 00:11:45.538
When you try to speak Dutch with people, 
they will probably answer you in English.

00:11:45.818 --> 00:11:49.618
But you can solve that problem by taking 
some lessons or doing a language exchange

00:11:49.618 --> 00:11:53.578
and practicing on your own until you
reach
a fairly good level and have a fairly good base

00:11:53.798 --> 00:11:57.578
Then, after that, you can speak to people in Dutch 
without totally pissing them off.

00:11:57.737 --> 00:12:00.337
 

00:12:00.438 --> 00:12:02.658
The question of the day for native speakers of Dutch:

00:12:02.857 --> 00:12:06.917
How well can you understand the speakers 
of regional languages that are related to Dutch?

00:12:07.097 --> 00:12:09.417
Languages like Frisian or Low Saxon.

00:12:09.578 --> 00:12:13.858
And for people who have studied Dutch: What was 
your experience with trying to learn and practice Dutch?

00:12:13.858 --> 00:12:16.198
How do people respond to you? 
Did they answer you in English?

00:12:16.298 --> 00:12:18.998
What was the best way for you 
to learn and practice Dutch?

